@namespace Fhi.Munin.Explorer.Blazor

@* Kelda, the kildeutforsker.

   No @page — the host owns routing; in helsedata's Optimizely CMS there is no router at all.
   No @rendermode — the host decides at the mount site, which is what lets this same component
   render under legacy Blazor Server and under a modern Blazor Web App.
   No CSS ships from this package.

   The names below split the way they do everywhere in this package. The page furniture wears
   Fhi.Helsedata.Stiler's own names — `headline`, `caption`, `form-element__label`,
   `searchbox__freetext*`, `hd-button-square` with its modifiers, `infobox` — and the structure
   wears the `munin-explorer` prefix this package owns. Five of those are the explorer's existing
   ones, reused rather than reinvented: the root `munin-explorer`, the `munin-explorer-container`
   and `munin-explorer-results` the list sits in, `munin-explorer-filters` for the facet panel and
   `munin-explorer-drilldown` for the view an opened kilde takes over. Others are this view's own —
   `munin-explorer-kilder` for the table, `munin-explorer-kilder__name` for the control that opens a
   row, `munin-explorer-kilder__count` for the columns holding a number with
   `munin-explorer-kilder__count--zero` on a cell whose count is nought,
   `munin-explorer-kilder__select` for the checkbox column in front of them,
   `munin-explorer-filters__toggle` and `munin-explorer-filters__facets` for the panel that folds
   away on a narrow screen, and `munin-explorer-filters__count` for the number beside a facet
   value.

   The result list is a <table>, unlike the variable explorer's, and that is the shape rule rather
   than an inconsistency. Runa's rows are helsedata's own `munin-explorer-data-list`, a <ul> with a
   header row of <div>s, because that is the shape their stylesheet dresses and this component
   replaces their variable page. There is no equivalent to copy for a list of kilder, and eight
   columns of counts and names have to line up — so it is the element that already does that, for
   the reason the kodeverk code list and KildeView's datasamlinger are tables too: an element
   degrades to its own browser default where an unknown class name degrades to nothing. The name
   is a <button> for the same reason.

   No pager and no sortable headers, and no URL state written from here. The first two are Kelda
   parity decisions recorded under the epic, not omissions — the endpoint is not paged, and the
   order is chosen from one control above the table rather than from thirteen column headings.
   The third is this component's contract: the order and the open kilde are what a host puts in
   its URL, and KildeExplorer is the mount that does it. See the component class.

   Accessibility: helsedata.no is a public-sector site, so WCAG 2.1 AA is a legal requirement, and
   this is our markup on their page. Visible focus and contrast are the host stylesheet's to get
   right; what markup can decide is decided here. *@

@* data-munin-explorer-version is not a class and no stylesheet can have a rule for it. It is the
   only way to read which version of this package is serving a host's page: the RCL renders through
   the host's circuit and ships no static asset to fingerprint. (Fhi.Metadata-sqbei) *@
<section class="munin-explorer"
         data-munin-explorer-version="@ExplorerVersion.Current"
         aria-labelledby="@TitleId">
    @* Rendered from HeadingLevel rather than hard-coded to h2: the level that keeps the document
       outline unbroken is only knowable at the mount site. *@
    @Heading

@if (_selectedId is not null)
{
    @* A kilde opens as its own view, not as a panel inside a row. Runa navigates to a page for
       this; this component has no router, so instead it takes over its own area and offers a way
       back. The search text, the ticked values and the list are all still there on return because
       every one of them is held here rather than in the DOM — the list was never refetched to begin
       with. The facet folds are the one exception, and deliberately: they belong to the <details>
       elements this branch replaces, so the panel comes back seeded at its own defaults. See the
       facets below.

       A region named after what it shows, so a screen reader moving by landmark hears which kilde
       it landed in. *@
    <div class="munin-explorer-drilldown"
         id="@DetailId"
         role="region"
         aria-labelledby="@DetailHeadingId"
         aria-busy="@DetailBusy">
        @* --ghost-blue for the same reason the variable explorer's way out wears it: a plain ghost
           shows nothing until :hover, and this is navigation. (Fhi.Metadata-l9l2n.34) *@
        <button class="hd-button-square button-square--ghost-blue margin-bottom"
                type="button"
                @onclick="CloseAsync">@T.BackToKilder</button>

        @* Keep the landmark named while the shared detail heading is still loading. *@
        @if (_kilde is null)
        {
            @DrilldownHeading
        }

        <p class="@DetailStatusClass"
           role="status"
           aria-live="polite"
           aria-atomic="true">@DetailStatus</p>

        @if (_kilde is { } kilde)
        {
            @* Only the additional sections belong to this explorer; KildeView owns the hierarchy. *@
            <KildeView Kilde="kilde"
                       Language="@Language"
                       HeadingLevel="@KildeLevel"
                       HeadingId="@DetailHeadingId">
                <Sections>
                    @* At SectionLevel rather than a literal h2: these have to land on the level
                       KildeView gives its own block headings, or "Variabler" reads as part of the
                       datasamlinger above it rather than as a section beside them. *@
                    @SectionHeading(T.HeadingVariables)
                    <p class="caption">@T.KildeVariableCount(kilde.TotalVariables)</p>

                    @* `caption` for the reason the count above wears it: a classless <p> picks no
                       size at all, so on a host carrying Stiler and nothing else these two would
                       land at the browser's own paragraph size beside a sibling that is dressed. *@
                    @if (ShowAccessAndPrices)
                    {
                        @SectionHeading(T.HeadingAccessCriteria)
                        <p class="caption">@T.BodyAccessCriteria</p>

                        @SectionHeading(T.HeadingPrices)
                        <p class="caption">@T.BodyPrices</p>
                    }

                    @* Last, and outside Kelda's own: whatever the host hung on this explorer. A
                       host's sections are additions to the page it embedded, so they come after the
                       sections the component itself is. *@
                    @Sections
                </Sections>
            </KildeView>
        }
    </div>
}
else
{
    @* The filter, read once for the whole render. Four things below want it — the count, the empty
       state, the table's own name and its rows — and the getter re-runs the Where and materialises
       a new list on every one of them. Nothing changes between them, so this is shape rather than
       correctness: it is how KildeView.DataCollectionTable opens (`var rows = DataCollections;`)
       and what the variable explorer's `@if (HierarchyCrumbs is { Count: > 0 } crumbs)` does. *@
    var visible = Visible;
    var summary = Summary(visible);
    var searchText = SearchText;

    @* role="search" makes this a search landmark, and aria-labelledby names it after this
       instance's title so two explorers on one page are told apart in a landmark list.

       There is nothing behind the submit — the filtering is done over a list already in hand — but
       the form is still a form: implicit submit on Enter is how a keyboard user finishes a search,
       and preventing the default is what keeps that from reloading the host's page. *@
    <form role="search"
          aria-labelledby="@TitleId"
          @onsubmit="Submit"
          @onsubmit:preventDefault="true">
        @* A visible label rather than a bare aria-label: `form-element__label` is Stiler's own
           label style, so keeping the text on screen costs nothing and serves far more people. *@
        <label class="form-element__label" for="@SearchId">@T.KildeSearchLabel</label>

        @* searchbox__freetext reserves the right-hand padding for the controls inside the field.
           The reservation and searchbox__freetext-submit-button are Stiler's, and their own search
           box is arranged this way; the clear control below borrows the room, not the name. *@
        <div class="searchbox__freetext-container">
            @* @bind (onchange), deliberately NOT value + @oninput.

               This renders inside helsedata.no's Blazor Server circuit, so @oninput means one
               round-trip per keystroke — traffic for nothing, multiplied by every visitor on a
               public site, and worse than nothing because each round-trip re-renders and rewrites
               the input while more input is still arriving: a fast paste or a programmatic fill
               loses characters. That the filtering itself is client-side does not change any of
               it; the round-trip is the event reaching the circuit at all. The event is spelled
               out rather than left implicit so nobody "helpfully" restores oninput.

               type="text", not type="search": the user-agent ✕ fires the DOM `search` event, which
               Blazor does not know, so it emptied the box without applying it. enterkeyhint keeps
               the soft keyboard's search key. (Fhi.Metadata-5ghur) *@
            <input class="searchbox__freetext"
                   id="@SearchId"
                   type="text"
                   enterkeyhint="search"
                   @ref="_searchField"
                   @bind="_search"
                   @bind:event="onchange"
                   placeholder="@T.KildeSearchPlaceholder"
                   autocomplete="off"
                   maxlength="200" />
            @* Drawn only when there is something to clear, so a press takes it off the page under
               the reader's focus — which is why the handler hands that focus back. Named by
               aria-label: the glyph is not a name. (Fhi.Metadata-ag4n7) *@
            @if (searchText is not null)
            {
                <button class="munin-explorer-search__clear"
                        type="button"
                        aria-label="@T.ClearSearch"
                        @onclick="ClearSearchAndRefocusAsync">&#xD7;</button>
            }
            @* Deliberately NOT disabled. Disabling the element that currently has focus drops
               focus to <body>, and this button's real job is the blur it causes: clicking it from
               inside the field is what fires the change event that applies the search. *@
            <button class="hd-button-square button-square--primary searchbox__freetext-submit-button"
                    type="submit">@T.SearchButton</button>
        </div>
    </form>

    @* The facets. Four of them at most — kildetype, kategori, tilgangsnivå and databehandler — and
       only the ones the catalogue actually has values for: a facet with no choices is not drawn at
       all, which is the one thing Munin's own Kelda gets wrong here. The counting, the ordering and
       the OR-within/AND-across rule are all in KildeSearch.Filters.cs.

       `munin-explorer-filters` is the explorer's existing handle for a facet panel, reused rather
       than reinvented — the sample stylesheets place it in a column beside the results. It is a
       hook and nothing more: neither this package nor Stiler defines a rule for it, which is what
       the variable explorer's own remarks say of it, so a host on Stiler that wants the panel
       placed writes that rule itself. Two names under it are this panel's own, and both exist
       because a narrow screen needs the panel folded away while a wide one does not: `__toggle` is
       the button that unfolds it, hidden once there is room for a sidebar, and `__facets` is what
       it unfolds. Those two are new here, so a Stiler host needs rules for them as well — the
       samples are the only stylesheet in this repository that has any.

       The folding itself is the `hidden` attribute rather than a class, so the panel works on a host
       that styles none of this: `hidden` is the browser's own, the button toggles it, and a host
       with a sidebar overrides it back to visible in one rule. That is the same trade the rest of
       this component makes — an element degrades to its own browser default, where a class name no
       stylesheet has heard of degrades to nothing.

       One <details> per facet, the shape the variable explorer's panel already has under this same
       handle: `.munin-explorer-filters details` and `summary` are dressed unscoped to either panel,
       in both sample stylesheets and in Stiler 0.1.42, so no new class name is introduced here.

       Checkboxes, not the toggle buttons the variable explorer's facets use. Kelda's are checkboxes,
       and a bare <input type="checkbox"> inside its own <label> is an element pair every stylesheet
       already dresses, so neither the box nor the label carries a class.

       The count does, `munin-explorer-filters__count` — a number that cannot be dimmed or aligned
       reads as more of the label's words. It goes inside the label, the separating space beside
       the span: see KildeSearch.Filters.cs. (Fhi.Metadata-cgk85) *@
    @if (Facets is { Count: > 0 } facets)
    {
        <div class="munin-explorer-filters">
            @FiltersHeading

            @* aria-expanded describes the attribute below it and nothing else. On a screen wide
               enough for a sidebar this button is display:none and the panel is shown regardless, so
               the pair is never read while it disagrees with what is on screen. *@
            <button class="hd-button-square button-square--ghost margin-bottom munin-explorer-filters__toggle"
                    type="button"
                    aria-expanded="@(_filtersOpen ? "true" : "false")"
                    aria-controls="@FacetsId"
                    @onclick="ToggleFilters">@(_filtersOpen ? T.HideFilters : T.ShowFilters)</button>

            <div class="munin-explorer-filters__facets" id="@FacetsId" hidden="@(!_filtersOpen)">
                @foreach (var facet in facets)
                {
                    @* IsSearchable over every option the facet has, never over what its own search leaves:
                       the second reading takes the box away the moment it works. Nor is the ternary that
                       twice: a facet fallen under the threshold holding a term would filter on with no box. *@
                    var searchable = IsSearchable(facet);
                    var options = searchable ? VisibleOptions(facet) : facet.Options;

                    @* A native <details> per facet, the shape the variable explorer's panel already
                       uses under this same handle. Keyed because the fold is the element's own: a
                       facet dropped from the middle would otherwise leave the one that slides up
                       into its place wearing whatever the reader had done to the old one. *@
                    <details @key="facet.Key" open="@facet.OpenByDefault">
                        @* <summary> is the disclosure control natively — focusable, Enter and Space
                           toggle it, its open attribute is the state, so there is no aria-expanded
                           to drift. The heading stays one: a panel is navigated by heading. *@
                        <summary>@FacetHeading(facet)</summary>

                        @* A search over this facet's own values, for a facet long enough to need
                           one. The threshold and the reasoning are in KildeSearch.Filters.cs.

                           No class name of its own, the same bargain the dataperiode facet's date
                           fields make: a bare <input> is an element every stylesheet already draws,
                           where a name Stiler has never heard of draws nothing at all.

                           The label is real and hidden rather than an aria-label, and it names the
                           facet, because several of these boxes can be on screen at once.

                           onchange, never oninput — one round-trip per keystroke on helsedata's
                           circuit whatever the handler does with it, and each one rewrites the
                           field while more input is arriving. (Fhi.Metadata-l9l2n.26)

                           No enterkeyhint, unlike the freetext box above: what earns that key there
                           is a <form> whose preventDefault makes Enter search rather than reload the
                           host's page, and this box has none to put it in.

                           @ref because committing rewrites the list beside it, under a reader who
                           committed with Tab and is standing in it — see SearchFacetAsync. *@
                        @if (searchable)
                        {
                            <label class="screenreader-only" for="@FacetSearchId(facet.Key)">@T.FacetSearchLabel(facet.Heading)</label>
                            <input id="@FacetSearchId(facet.Key)"
                                   type="text"
                                   @ref="_facetSearchFields[facet.Key]"
                                   value="@FacetSearchValue(facet.Key)"
                                   @onchange="e => SearchFacetAsync(facet.Key, e.Value as string)"
                                   placeholder="@T.FacetSearchPlaceholder"
                                   autocomplete="off"
                                   maxlength="200" />
                        }

                        @* Rendered whether or not the facet is open, and hidden by the disclosure
                           itself rather than by us. `open` is the same on every render — the facets
                           are counted over the unfiltered list — so Blazor writes it once and never
                           again, and the fold is the reader's for as long as these elements live:
                           narrowing cannot collapse what they opened, and each facet folds alone.
                           They do not survive a drill-in, which removes the panel; the ticks and
                           the search do, so the reader loses only the shape of the panel.

                           `options` is what the facet's own search leaves. It must never reach the
                           ticks: a choice typed out of sight is still ticked and still narrowing
                           the list, and clearing the box brings it back with its tick on. *@
                        @* Said rather than left as an empty list: a facet that has drawn nothing
                           reads as one whose values failed to load. `caption` is Stiler's muted
                           secondary text, the class the list's own status line wears. *@
                        @if (options.Count == 0)
                        {
                            <p class="caption">@T.FacetSearchNoMatch</p>
                        }
                        else
                        {
                            <ul>
                                @foreach (var option in options)
                                {
                                    @* The whole value in title, and only where the text on screen was
                                       cut — one databehandler on the live catalogue is over 200
                                       characters of free text, and a reader who wants to know which one
                                       it is has nowhere else to read it. Keyed on the value: the options are built
                                       off the unfiltered list so they never move, and the key is what
                                       keeps that true if they ever do. *@
                                    <li @key="option.Value">
                                        @* lang where the choice is drawn in the catalogue's own words
                                           rather than in this package's — databehandler, which is free
                                           text, and any token no vocabulary had a word for. It is the
                                           same marking the table's cells carry for the same strings,
                                           and it covers the title as well as the text. See
                                           KildeSearch.Filters.cs for which labels are which. *@
                                        <label title="@option.Title" lang="@option.Language">
                                            <input type="checkbox"
                                                   checked="@IsChosen(facet.Key, option.Value)"
                                                   @onchange="e => Choose(facet.Key, option.Value, e.Value is true)" />
                                            @option.Text@(" ")<span class="munin-explorer-filters__count">@option.CountText</span>
                                        </label>
                                    </li>
                                }
                            </ul>
                        }
                    </details>
                }
            </div>
        </div>
    }

    @* One polite status line, holding one message at a time: loading, the count, or the empty
       state. aria-atomic makes a screen reader read the whole new sentence rather than only the
       words that changed — "12" on its own is not information.

       `caption` is Stiler's muted secondary-text style. It carries the empty state too: a
       full-page emptystate block would be out of proportion inside a component that is one section
       of somebody else's page, and repeating the sentence outside the live region would put it on
       screen twice. *@
    <p class="caption" role="status" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true">
        @if (_loading)
        {
            @T.KildeListLoading
        }
        else if (visible.Count > 0)
        {
            @summary
        }
        else if (_loaded && _error is null)
        {
            @* Two sentences rather than one: a catalogue with nothing in it and a search that
               matched nothing ask the reader to do different things. The second names the facets
               as well, because with a facet ticked the reader has narrowed the list twice and a
               sentence blaming only the search would send them to fix the wrong thing. *@
            @(_kilder.Count == 0 ? T.NoKilder : T.NoKilderMatch(searchText, ChosenCount))
        }
    </p>

    @* The failure is worth interrupting for, so it is an alert rather than a polite update. The
       container is always rendered, empty when there is nothing wrong: a role="alert" element that
       is inserted and filled in the same DOM update is announced unreliably, while one that is
       already there when its text arrives is not. It carries no class of its own precisely because
       it is always present — an `infobox` here would draw an empty coloured box on every page that
       has nothing to report. *@
    <div role="alert" aria-live="assertive" aria-atomic="true">
        @if (_error is not null)
        {
            <p class="infobox infobox--bg-yellow">@_error</p>
        }
    </div>

    @* Drawn only where the host wired ExploreVariablesRequested — a column with nowhere to go is
       worse than no column. The count is rendered while empty on purpose: a live region filled in
       the same update that inserts it is announced unreliably. (Fhi.Metadata-5ghur) *@
    @if (Selectable && (visible.Count > 0 || TickedCount > 0))
    {
        @* Handover, then reset, then count: everything that comes and goes is to the right of
           everything that does not, so ticking a row moves nothing the reader is aiming at. The
           handover's width is held by a host rule, since its label is one of three. (Fhi.Metadata-5ghur) *@
        <div class="munin-explorer-selection">
            <button class="hd-button-square button-square--primary munin-explorer-selection__explore"
                    type="button"
                    @onclick="() => ExploreVariablesAsync(visible)">@ExploreLabel</button>

            @if (TickedCount > 0)
            {
                <button class="hd-button-square button-square--secondary"
                        type="button"
                        @onclick="ClearTicks">@T.ClearSelection</button>
            }

            <p class="caption margin--none" role="status" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true">
                @(TickedCount > 0 ? T.SelectedKildeCount(TickedCount) : null)
            </p>
        </div>
    }

    @if (visible.Count > 0)
    {
    <div class="munin-explorer-container">
    <div class="munin-explorer-results">
        @* The order the list is in. Above the table with the column picker, and drawn on the same
           condition: an order control over no rows orders nothing.

           A <select> and not the proposal's chips: the mockup styles its own names from an inline
           <style> block, so nothing in it carries across, and an element degrades to its own
           browser default where a class no stylesheet has heard of degrades to nothing.

           The select and the wrapper both go unclassed. The form-element family in
           `test/host-class-names.txt` is __label, __sublabel, __caption and --error with nothing
           for the control itself, which is why the pager's size control is bare too; and
           `munin-explorer-results` is a column flex box that would stretch a classless child to
           the width of the table. The label wears Stiler's `form-element__label`, the same name
           the search box above it does. *@
        <div>
            <label class="form-element__label" for="@SortSelectId">@T.SortBy</label>

            @* `selected` on the option rather than `value` on the select, for the reason the size
               control gives: `value` is a DOM property the interactive renderer sets, so a
               statically rendered first paint would show the first order whatever the host asked
               for — and this one arrives from a link. *@
            @* Keyed on the refusal count, the same reason the size control is: a browser moves a
               select's own selection before `onchange` runs, and a refused value that leaves the
               order where it was renders nothing to move it back. See _orderRefusals. *@
            <select id="@SortSelectId" @key="_orderRefusals" @onchange="ChooseOrderAsync">
                @foreach (var offered in OfferedOrders)
                {
                    <option value="@offered" selected="@(_order == offered)">@T.KildeOrderLabel(offered)</option>
                }
            </select>
        </div>

        @* The column picker, above the table and inside the results column, which is where the
           variable explorer puts its own and where helsedata's page puts theirs. Drawn only with
           rows on screen, unlike that one: there the header also carries the ordering a reader
           pressed, and here an empty list means the fetch failed or the search matched nothing, so
           the control would be offering columns for a table that is not there. *@
        @ColumnPicker()

        @* Around the table alone: the column picker shares `munin-explorer-results` and would
           scroll out of reach with it. tabindex is unconditional because detecting overflow needs
           script; region and table share one name so neither is nameless. (Fhi.Metadata-b3brc) *@
        <div class="munin-explorer-kilder-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="@summary">
            @* aria-label rather than aria-labelledby: the name is the same sentence the status line
               above shows, and pointing at that element would make the table's name change under a
               screen reader every time the status line does.

               Thirteen columns in the order Kelda's own header draws them (kelda.tsx:479-535): Navn,
               Status and Opprettet always, and ten behind the picker above, of which Kelda's
               DEFAULT_VISIBLE turns three on.

               The picker lists those ten in a DIFFERENT order (kelda.tsx:74) — counts before free
               text, where the table puts free text first — and that is Kelda's, not a slip on either
               side. Copying one order and not the other would make this table Kelda's in the control
               and not in the columns.

               Two control columns come before all of them: the expand toggle, which is ours alone, and
               the checkbox, which is Kelda's and is drawn only where the host wired the handover. Both
               count towards the nested row's colspan.

               No aria-busy, unlike the variable explorer's list: there is nothing to be busy with.
               The list is fetched once, on initialisation, and while that fetch is running this table
               is not in the DOM at all — so the attribute could only ever have said "false". It is the
               drilldown, which does refetch on every kilde opened, that carries one. *@
            <table class="munin-explorer-kilder" aria-label="@summary">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        @* A control column with no visible heading: the name is there for a screen
                           reader, which announces it when it reaches the toggle below. *@
                        <th scope="col" class="munin-explorer-kilder__expand">
                            <span class="screenreader-only">@T.ColumnExpand</span>
                        </th>
                        @if (Selectable)
                        {
                            @* Over the rows the reader can see, and only those. No indeterminate state:
                               it is a DOM property with no attribute behind it, and the count above says
                               the same thing in words. (Fhi.Metadata-5ghur) *@
                            <th scope="col" class="munin-explorer-kilder__select">
                                <input type="checkbox"
                                       aria-label="@T.SelectAllKilder"
                                       checked="@AllVisibleTicked(visible)"
                                       @onchange="() => TickAllVisible(visible)" />
                            </th>
                        }
                        <th scope="col">@T.FieldName</th>
                        @if (ColumnVisible(KildeColumn.Kildetype))
                        {
                            <th scope="col">@T.ColumnKildetype</th>
                        }
                        <th scope="col">@T.FieldStatus</th>
                        @if (ColumnVisible(KildeColumn.DataController))
                        {
                            <th scope="col">@T.FieldDataController</th>
                        }
                        @if (ColumnVisible(KildeColumn.DataProcessor))
                        {
                            <th scope="col">@T.FieldDataProcessor</th>
                        }
                        @if (ColumnVisible(KildeColumn.PersonIdentification))
                        {
                            <th scope="col">@T.FieldPersonIdentification</th>
                        }
                        @if (ColumnVisible(KildeColumn.Validity))
                        {
                            <th scope="col">@T.FieldValidity</th>
                        }
                        @if (ColumnVisible(KildeColumn.Delkilder))
                        {
                            <th scope="col" class="munin-explorer-kilder__count">@T.ColumnDelkildeCount</th>
                        }
                        @if (ColumnVisible(KildeColumn.Datasamlinger))
                        {
                            <th scope="col" class="munin-explorer-kilder__count">@T.HeadingDataCollections</th>
                        }
                        @if (ColumnVisible(KildeColumn.Variabler))
                        {
                            <th scope="col" class="munin-explorer-kilder__count">@T.ColumnVariableCount</th>
                        }
                        @* No __count on this one: it holds a year the catalogue wrote down as text,
                           not a number this component counted, and the class right-aligns figures. *@
                        <th scope="col">@T.ColumnEstablished</th>
                        @if (ColumnVisible(KildeColumn.Imported))
                        {
                            <th scope="col">@T.ColumnImported</th>
                        }
                        @if (ColumnVisible(KildeColumn.SourceUpdated))
                        {
                            <th scope="col">@T.ColumnSourceUpdated</th>
                        }
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    @foreach (var kilde in visible)
                    {
                        @* Keyed by the kilde's own id: the rows come and go as the reader types, and
                           one of them holds the control the reader is standing on. Without a key the
                           renderer would patch that button into a different kilde's rather than moving
                           it. *@
                        <tr @key="kilde.Id">
                            @* Only where there is something to open. Kelda draws no toggle on a
                               kilde with no datasamlinger, and a control that expands to nothing
                               is worse than no control. *@
                            <td class="munin-explorer-kilder__expand">
                                @if (kilde.DatasamlingCount > 0)
                                {
                                    @* aria-controls only while the panel exists: an IDREF to an element
                                       that is not in the DOM is not a relationship, it is a dangling
                                       pointer that an a11y linter and a screen reader both follow. *@
                                    <button type="button"
                                            class="hd-button-reset munin-explorer-kilder__expand-toggle"
                                            aria-expanded="@(IsExpanded(kilde.Id) ? "true" : "false")"
                                            aria-controls="@(IsExpanded(kilde.Id) ? PanelId(kilde.Id) : null)"
                                            aria-label="@ExpandLabel(kilde)"
                                            @onclick="() => ToggleDatasamlingerAsync(kilde)">
                                        @* Decorative: aria-expanded above already says which way it
                                           points, and the button is named for the kilde it opens.
                                           Why a chevron rather than a "+" is on ExpandChevronClass. *@
                                        <span class="@ExpandChevronClass(kilde.Id)" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                                    </button>
                                }
                            </td>
                            @if (Selectable)
                            {
                                @* A td, not a th: the box is a control over the row, not its label. Its
                                   accessible name carries the kilde's, because "Velg" repeated down a
                                   column says nothing about which row. (Fhi.Metadata-5ghur) *@
                                <td class="munin-explorer-kilder__select">
                                    <input type="checkbox"
                                           aria-label="@T.SelectKilde(RowName(kilde).Text)"
                                           checked="@IsTicked(kilde.Id)"
                                           @onchange="e => Tick(kilde.Id, e.Value is true)" />
                                </td>
                            }
                            @* The name is a th, not a td: it is what the rest of the row is about, and
                               a screen reader reading a cell out of context should hear which kilde it
                               belongs to. The catalogue holds one name, in Norwegian, so it is marked
                               when the reader is not. *@
                            <th scope="row">
                                <button class="munin-explorer-kilder__name"
                                        type="button"
                                        lang="@CatalogueProperties.Foreign(RowName(kilde).Norwegian, Reader)"
                                        @onclick="() => SelectAsync(kilde)">@RowName(kilde).Text</button>

                                @* The code under the name, the way Kelda puts it there — it is how a reader
                                   who knows K_ALS finds the row whose name they do not know. Not drawn
                                   when the button above has already fallen back to it. *@
                                @if (RowName(kilde).Norwegian)
                                {
                                    <p class="caption margin--none">@kilde.Code</p>
                                }
                            </th>
                            @if (ColumnVisible(KildeColumn.Kildetype))
                            {
                                <td>@T.KildeTypeLabel(kilde.Kildetype, kilde.Kildetype)</td>
                            }
                            <td>@(kilde.IsActive ? T.StatusActive : T.StatusPassive)</td>
                            @* CatalogueLang and not Foreign: both are free text the catalogue wrote in Norwegian,
                               but an empty one is drawn as our own "Ikke oppgitt", and a lang the
                               content is not in is worse than none (WCAG 3.1.2). *@
                            @if (ColumnVisible(KildeColumn.DataController))
                            {
                                <td lang="@CatalogueLang(kilde.DataController)">@Value(kilde.DataController)</td>
                            }
                            @if (ColumnVisible(KildeColumn.DataProcessor))
                            {
                                <td lang="@CatalogueLang(kilde.DataProcessor)">@Value(kilde.DataProcessor)</td>
                            }
                            @if (ColumnVisible(KildeColumn.PersonIdentification))
                            {
                                <td>@T.PersonIdentificationLabel(kilde.PersonIdentificationLevel)</td>
                            }
                            @if (ColumnVisible(KildeColumn.Validity))
                            {
                                <td>@ValidityPeriod(kilde)</td>
                            }
                            @* The count still reads "0" — CountClass only adds a modifier, so a reader
                               can tell a register that holds nothing from one nobody filled in, which
                               is what "Ikke oppgitt" says in the columns beside these. *@
                            @if (ColumnVisible(KildeColumn.Delkilder))
                            {
                                <td class="@CountClass(kilde.DelkildeCount)">@kilde.DelkildeCount</td>
                            }
                            @if (ColumnVisible(KildeColumn.Datasamlinger))
                            {
                                <td class="@CountClass(kilde.DatasamlingCount)">@kilde.DatasamlingCount</td>
                            }
                            @if (ColumnVisible(KildeColumn.Variabler))
                            {
                                <td class="@CountClass(kilde.TotalVariables)">@kilde.TotalVariables</td>
                            }
                            @* No lang here, unlike the name above: the catalogue writes this field as
                               a four-digit year, which has no language to mark (WCAG 3.1.2), and where
                               it is missing the cell holds our "Ikke oppgitt" in the reader's own. *@
                            <td>@Value(Established(kilde))</td>
                            @if (ColumnVisible(KildeColumn.Imported))
                            {
                                <td>@Imported(kilde)</td>
                            }
                            @if (ColumnVisible(KildeColumn.SourceUpdated))
                            {
                                <td>@SourceUpdated(kilde)</td>
                            }
                        </tr>
                        @if (IsExpanded(kilde.Id))
                        {
                            @* Its own row rather than a region inside the one above: a table row
                               cannot hold a block, and the panel has to span every column. *@
                            <tr @key="@($"{kilde.Id}-datasamlinger")">
                                <td colspan="@RowSpan" id="@PanelId(kilde.Id)" class="munin-explorer-kilder__expanded">
                                    @* One live region for both states, as the drilldown does above and
                                       the variable explorer's panel does: the panel is inserted after a
                                       press, so a line that is not announced is a line nobody hears. *@
                                    <p class="@ExpandedStatusClass(kilde.Id)"
                                       role="status"
                                       aria-live="polite"
                                       aria-atomic="true">@ExpandedStatus(kilde.Id)</p>

                                    @if (!_datasamlingerLoading.Contains(kilde.Id) && !_datasamlingerError.ContainsKey(kilde.Id))
                                    {
                                        @PanelHeading(RowName(kilde).Text)

                                        @foreach (var group in DatasamlingGroups(kilde.Id))
                                        {
                                            @if (group.Heading is { } heading)
                                            {
                                                @GroupHeading(heading, group.Code, group.Depth)
                                            }
                                            @DatasamlingTable.For(group.Rows, T, Language, Reader)
                                        }
                                    }
                                </td>
                            </tr>
                        }
                    }
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </div>
    </div>
    }
}
</section>
